INT. RADIO STATION BOOTH - NIGHT

A small glass room above a shuttered strip mall. Dials, cables, dead plants. A red ON AIR sign glows above the window. Beyond it, the darkened studio floor. Out the far window, a truck stop's neon bleeds into the rain.

LEANDRO PACHECO, 40s, the overnight host, headphones around his neck, a thermos of coffee at his elbow, leans back in a creaking chair. He moves the faders with the unthinking ease of fifteen years. A pile of unopened mail beside him.

LEANDRO
(into the mic, smooth)
That was Patsy Cline, and you're listening to Night Drive on 94.7. Four-fifteen in the morning. Phone lines are open.

He flicks a switch. A line blinks red.

LEANDRO (CONT'D)
First caller. You're on the air.

CALLER (V.O.)
(over the phone, very quiet)
Hello, Leandro.

LEANDRO
Hello. What can I play for you tonight?

CALLER (V.O.)
Nothing. I just wanted to hear you talk.

LEANDRO
(a small laugh)
Well. That's what I'm here for. What's your name?

CALLER (V.O.)
You know my name.

Leandro's smile fades by a degree. He glances at the line indicator: caller ID reads NO SIGNAL.

LEANDRO
I'm afraid I don't. Help me out.

CALLER (V.O.)
It's been so long since you said it.

He sits up. Takes a slow sip of coffee. Sets the mug down without a sound.

LEANDRO
Let's go to a song, shall we? This one's for you, friend.

He thumbs the fader. A soft ballad swells. He mutes the mic, but the line is still open. He doesn't hang up.

CALLER (V.O.)
Are you still afraid of the water, Leandro?

His hand freezes. The headphones slide off his shoulder to the floor.

LEANDRO
(low)
Who is this?

CALLER (V.O.)
Look behind you.

He doesn't. He stares at his reflection in the glass: the booth, the lamp, the empty studio beyond. And behind his reflection, standing in the dark at the edge of the floor, a figure he cannot make out.

He reaches for the ON AIR switch. Turns it off. The red glow dies.

The line goes dead. The song plays on to an empty room.
